      "explanation": "Both equations contain the same factor \\(k\\), so division will eliminate it. Since \\(2^r>0\\), the first equation gives \\(k>0\\), making this division valid. Dividing the second equation by the first gives\n\\[\n5=\\frac{15}{3}\n=\\frac{k4^r}{k2^r}\n=\\frac{2^{2r}}{2^r}\n=2^r.\n\\]\nBy definition, \\(\\log_2 5\\) is the exponent to which \\(2\\) must be raised to obtain \\(5\\). This value is feasible: with \\(k=3/5\\), the two original right-hand sides are \\(3\\) and \\(15\\). Therefore \\(r=\\log_2 5\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
